#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n2. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28$. Likewise,\n$P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(0, 1)|= 0.12 = 0.4 \\cdot 0.3 = f_{X}(0) \\cdot f_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1, 0)|= 0.42 = 0.6 \\cdot 0.7 = f_{X}(1) \\cdot f_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1, 1)|= 0.18 = 0.6 \\cdot 0.3 = f_{X}(1) \\cdot f_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/id #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12463" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y)$, find $f(4,2)$.\n\n| $x$$1$$2$$3$$4$$f_X(x)$$0.4$$0.3$$0.2$$0.1$ |  | $y$$2$$4$$f_Y(y)$$0.55$$0.45$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/id #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12464" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.56$ | $0.14$ |\n\n| $x$$0$$1$$f_X(x)$$0.3$$0.7$ |  | $y$$0$$1$$f_Y(y)$$0.8$$0.2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n2. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.24$. On the other hand,\n$P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.3 \\cdot 0.8 = 0.24. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(1, 0)|= 0.56 = 0.7 \\cdot 0.8 = f_{X}(1) \\cdot f_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0, 1)|= 0.06 = 0.3 \\cdot 0.2 = f_{X}(0) \\cdot f_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1, 1)|= 0.14 = 0.7 \\cdot 0.2 = f_{X}(1) \\cdot f_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30d7ab4-10f5-5989-8ec2-ec2450038780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158959} {:answer/id #uuid "a68b8f71-35a7-5bd6-88c9-f76507cf3663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158960} {:answer/id #uuid "d838d916-561d-559f-ab56-d23ff5056d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158961} {:answer/id #uuid "078da2a7-4e8e-57e1-828d-d09cdc3042e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158962} {:answer/